df45d975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097784 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097785 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097785 #edb/ref 1061 777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097785 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60f88dd0-c1bd-546f-9dac-f440401fd02b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097785 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097786 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097786 #edb/ref 1061 780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097786 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90637184-1911-5cfb-bf2c-dbdad69086a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097786 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097787 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097787 #edb/ref 1061 781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097787 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35489675-a0a1-59ea-b1f7-ab794d855f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097787 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097807 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{'}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{'}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{'}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097807 #edb/ref 1065 13330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097807 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d34fc877-5572-53a2-b957-2c5d44a488f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097807 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097872 #edb/ref 1064 "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097872 #edb/ref 1065 415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097872 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097872 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097873 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097873 #edb/ref 1065 418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097873 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58c36c82-b739-55f9-8e99-4df1e0cbde24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097873 #edb/ref 1067 "Another Notation for the Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493329 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493330 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={(4x+6)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c698fb8-2f47-52aa-ab20-d02b17699d11" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493330 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $g(x)=4x+6$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+6{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+6{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493328 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493331 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{5x-3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97e8ab80-425a-5ce3-aeca-1b6c32080f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493331 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $g(x)=5x-3$ and $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{g(x)}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\sqrt{g})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(5x-3)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493333 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493332 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a645231c-c79c-561b-9b41-b96c53332e19" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493332 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $g(x)={x}^{5}-7{x}^{4}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})^{′}=5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{5}-7{x}^{4})(\\,5{x}^{4}-28{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493336 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186096843.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186493336 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "f62990ef-82f5-5028-b79d-43e7f23bfce3" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
